Matt Levine's Money Stuff column "Attention is all you need" [](cite:levine2026) argues that agentic artificial intelligence will reshape retail-facing financial businesses by changing how much attention customers pay to their finances. This writeup follows that argument and supplies the financial background the column assumes.

The argument draws on several concepts that are explained elsewhere. On the side of the banks, these are the [net interest margin](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/) and the deposit spread behind it, which are measured in [basis points](../../concepts/basis-points/). On the side of the brokerages, they are [payment for order flow](../../concepts/payment-for-order-flow/), which rests in turn on [market making](../../concepts/market-making/) and [adverse selection](../../concepts/adverse-selection/), together with [brokerage cash balance revenue](../../concepts/brokerage-cash-balance-revenue/) and [prediction markets and sportsbooks](../../concepts/prediction-markets/). Two further concepts, the dispersion trade and the wheel strategy, are explained in sections of their own below.

# Dispersion trade

A dispersion trade is a strategy that bets that outcomes within a group of related assets will diverge, some performing well while others perform poorly, and will not all move in the same direction. It takes no view on the direction of a sector or market as a whole. It takes a view on the _differences within_ it, and it profits when predictions about relative performance are correct, whether the group as a whole goes up or down.

The literal version of the trade lives in derivatives markets. Consider a trader who looks at the technology stocks of the S&P 500 and concludes that the sector as a whole will not move much, but that individual stocks will swing widely in different directions, some reporting blowout earnings while others miss expectations. The trader could sell options on an index of the sector, betting that the index will remain relatively stable, while buying options on the individual stocks, betting that each of them will move significantly. If the individual stocks move in offsetting directions, some rising sharply and others falling sharply, the index stays roughly flat and the options sold on it expire harmlessly, while the options on the individual stocks gain value from the large moves. The trader profits from the _dispersion_ of returns across the group.

What the trade is really exposed to is correlation. An index that holds its stocks with weights $w_i$, where stock $i$ has volatility $\sigma_i$ and the returns of stocks $i$ and $j$ have correlation $\rho_{ij}$ (with $\rho_{ii} = 1$), has a volatility $\sigma_I$ given by

$$
	\sigma_I^2 = \sum_{i} \sum_{j} w_i w_j \rho_{ij} \sigma_i \sigma_j. @tag(index-variance)
$$

In the simplest case of $n$ equally weighted stocks, each with the same volatility $\sigma$ and every pair with the same correlation $\rho$, [](eq:index-variance) reduces to

$$
	\sigma_I = \sigma \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} + \Big(1 - \frac{1}{n}\Big)\rho}.
$$

<IndexVolatility />

When the stocks all move together, with $\rho$ near one, the index is as volatile as its members, and options on the members gain nothing that options on the index do not. When they move independently of one another, their moves largely cancel inside the index: twenty stocks with a volatility of 30% each and a pairwise correlation of $0.2$ make an index with a volatility of only about 15%. The prices of index options and of single-stock options together imply a level of correlation, and a dispersion trade is, at its core, a bet that correlation will turn out lower than that: that individual outcomes will be more spread out, more dispersed, than current pricing implies.

The term is also used more broadly, as a mental model for any investment thesis that identifies winners and losers within a single theme. A dispersion thesis does not say that a trend is good for a whole industry. It says that the trend will benefit some participants and harm others within the same industry, and that the divergence can be traded. A rise in interest rates, for example, might be good for banks that earn large [net interest margins](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/) but bad for mortgage lenders whose refinancing volume dries up. An investor with this view would buy bank stocks and sell mortgage lender stocks, a dispersion trade on the theme of rising rates.

The key characteristic of any dispersion trade is that it is _market-neutral_ with respect to the overall theme. The sector need not go up or down for the trade to work. Only the predictions about which participants win and which lose must be correct. This makes dispersion trades appealing to those with strong views about relative performance within a group but less conviction about the group's overall direction. The risk is that the winners and losers are misidentified, or that an unexpected shock moves the entire group together and overwhelms the relative positions.

# A dispersion trade on attention

The author frames the entire argument as a dispersion trade. No literal derivatives position is described. The concept serves as a mental model for how agentic artificial intelligence will create winners and losers within the financial sector.

The thesis splits retail-facing financial businesses into two categories. The first holds businesses that profit when customers pay _too little_ attention: banks, insurers, mortgage companies. These businesses are built on customer inertia, and an agent that tirelessly optimizes a customer's finances threatens their revenue. The second holds businesses that profit when customers pay _more_ attention, or at least generate more activity: retail brokerages, sportsbooks, prediction markets. These businesses benefit when agents trade more frequently on behalf of customers. The author's trade is to be bearish on the first group and bullish on the second. The framing is what makes the argument more interesting than the observation that artificial intelligence will change finance: within a single theme, retail finance, the same technology creates opposing forces according to which side of the attention trade a business sits on.

# How banks profit from inattention

See also: [Net interest margin](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/)

The column assumes an understanding of how banks earn money from deposits. A bank lends or invests the money deposited with it at market rates and pays its depositors less, and the difference is its [deposit spread](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/#the-deposit-spread), one of the largest sources of profit in traditional banking. A savings account might pay 0.5% while the bank earns 4% or 5% on the money.

The spread depends on customer inattention. When market interest rates rise, deposit rates do not rise to match, a lag measured by the [deposit beta](../../concepts/net-interest-margin/#deposit-beta). Most customers do not shop around for the best savings rate, refinance their mortgage when rates drop, or move money out of a low-yield checking account. The time, effort, and attention that doing so requires mean that most customers leave their money where it is, and the bank profits from the inertia. The author calls this a "laziness tax" and notes that Americans forgo "untold sums" of money every year because of it.

The column's argument is that an agent acting on a customer's behalf would have no such inertia. It would continuously compare the rate a bank pays against competitors and market alternatives, and it would move money to whichever account offers the best return. This compresses the bank's deposit spread: the bank must either pay depositors closer to market rates, reducing its margin, or watch deposits leave. The author refers to "agentic bank runs", a scenario in which agents collectively pull deposits out of banks that offer below-market rates, potentially destabilizing banks that rely on sticky deposits for funding.

The author's phrase for the result is that an agent will "extract the last basis point from banks". A [basis point](../../concepts/basis-points/) is one hundredth of a percentage point, the unit in which small differences in rates are stated, and on a household's savings a single basis point is worth very little: \$1 a year on \$10,000. The point is that an agent has effectively unlimited attention and energy, so it will pursue gains that are too small for a person to bother with.

A similar logic applies to other products built on customer inertia. Life insurance policies, annuities, and mortgages are all priced with assumptions about how many customers will actively shop around, refinance, or switch providers. If agents reduce that friction, the businesses that depend on it lose revenue.

# How retail brokerages profit from attention

See also: [Payment for order flow](../../concepts/payment-for-order-flow/), [Brokerage cash balance revenue](../../concepts/brokerage-cash-balance-revenue/)

The second leg of the trade, the businesses that benefit from increased customer attention, requires an understanding of how modern retail brokerages in the United States make money, which the author only sketches. There are two main revenue streams.

The first is [payment for order flow](../../concepts/payment-for-order-flow/). A trade placed on an application like Robinhood does not go directly to a stock exchange. The brokerage routes it to a [market maker](../../concepts/market-making/), a firm such as Citadel Securities or Virtu Financial that continuously quotes prices at which it will buy and sell, and that earns the spread between them. In exchange for a steady stream of retail orders, the market maker pays the brokerage a fee.

Market makers pay for retail orders in particular because of [adverse selection](../../concepts/adverse-selection/), the risk of trading against someone who knows more. A hedge fund that has researched a company in depth is a dangerous counterparty. A retail customer trading on sentiment, headlines, or personal financial needs is a safe one, and so [retail order flow](../../concepts/adverse-selection/#retail-and-institutional-order-flow) is the most profitable kind a market maker can receive. The author illustrates this with a line from an earlier column: "for equity market makers, there are few more beautiful phrases in the English language than 'day trader and volleyball-programs coordinator.'" The ideal customer is someone who trades actively, generating many trades and thus many spreads, but has no informational edge. A part-time trader with a day job is perfect: active enough to generate revenue, uninformed enough to be a safe counterparty.

The second stream is [cash balance revenue](../../concepts/brokerage-cash-balance-revenue/). A brokerage invests the cash that sits uninvested in customer accounts, pays the customers a below-market rate on it, often close to zero, and keeps the difference. This is the deposit spread of a bank under another name, and unlike payment for order flow it moves _against_ attention: an attentive customer, or an agent, would move idle cash to a higher-yielding account.

Retail brokerages are therefore a mixed case. Revenue from order flow increases with attention, since more trades mean more revenue, while revenue from cash balances decreases with it. On net, the author appears to think the first effect dominates, which would explain why brokerages are embracing agents enthusiastically.

# Why trading agents suit brokerages

The column emphasizes that the profitability of retail trading for market makers, and thus for brokerages, is a matter not only of volume but of the _type_ of trading. Options, cryptocurrencies, and prediction-market contracts are especially lucrative because their bid-ask spreads are wider than those of plain stocks. Options involve more complexity and uncertainty, so market makers build in a larger margin of safety. Cryptocurrency markets are fragmented and volatile, producing wider spreads still. Prediction markets and sports bets carry very wide margins because the outcomes are highly uncertain and the retail customer has little informational advantage.

An agent that trades these instruments continuously, while the customer sleeps, works, or is otherwise disengaged, is ideal for the brokerage and the market maker. It generates high volume, trades in high-margin products, and does so without any informational edge that would create adverse selection. The author stresses the point: "enabling investors to trade … options, bitcoin and prediction-market contracts even when they are not paying attention" is described as "the Holy Grail of retail finance".

# The wheel strategy

The column mentions the wheel strategy as an example of what an agent might automate on Robinhood. The wheel is a popular options strategy designed to generate regular income from option premiums. It is a mechanical, rules-based approach that cycles between selling put options and selling call options on a stock, and it appeals to retail investors who want steady income from options without taking large directional bets.

The strategy is built from the two basic kinds of [option](../../concepts/european-option/): a put, which gives its buyer the right to sell a stock at a fixed strike price by an expiration date, and a call, which gives the right to buy. The seller of either, its writer, collects a premium in cash at the outset for taking on the obligation, and each contract covers 100 shares.

The wheel operates in two steps that repeat in a cycle.

**Step 1: sell a cash-secured put.** The trader chooses a stock that they would be willing to own and writes a put on it. The put is _cash-secured_ because the trader sets aside enough cash to buy the stock if the option is exercised. Suppose shares of Acme Corp are trading at \$100. The trader sells one put with a strike price of \$95 and an expiration one month away, for a premium of \$2.00 per share, or \$200 in total, and sets aside \$9,500. Two outcomes are possible. If Acme stays above \$95 through expiration, the put expires worthless: the trader keeps the \$200 premium, the \$9,500 is released, and Step 1 repeats. If Acme falls to \$93 at expiration, the put is exercised: the trader is obliged to buy 100 shares at \$95 each, spending the \$9,500, and now owns stock worth \$9,300. The unrealized loss of \$200 on the shares is offset by the \$200 premium collected. The trader moves to Step 2.

**Step 2: sell a covered call.** Now in possession of the stock, the trader writes a call on it. Because the trader owns the shares, which can be delivered if the option is exercised, this is a _covered_ call. Continuing the example, the trader holds Acme at a cost of \$95 per share and the stock is trading at \$93. The trader sells one call with a strike price of \$97 and an expiration one month away, for a premium of \$1.50 per share, or \$150. Again, two outcomes are possible. If Acme stays below \$97 through expiration, the call expires worthless: the trader keeps the \$150 premium, still owns the stock, and repeats Step 2. If Acme rises to \$99 at expiration, the call is exercised: the trader sells the 100 shares at \$97, receiving \$9,700, keeps the \$150 premium along with a \$200 gain on the shares, no longer owns the stock, and returns to Step 1.

<WheelPayoff />

The wheel generates income from option premiums on a regular basis, typically weekly or monthly according to the expirations chosen. In the example, the first cycle generated \$200 in premium, and the second generated \$150 in premium plus a \$200 capital gain, for a total of \$550 over two months.

The figure shows what the income is paid for. In both steps the trader's profit is capped above the strike and falls dollar for dollar with the stock below it. The main risk of the wheel is therefore a significant decline in the stock price: if Acme falls from \$100 to \$70 while the trader is holding it, the loss of \$25 per share on the stock, \$2,500 on 100 shares, far exceeds the few hundred dollars of premium collected. The strategy works best on stable or slowly rising stocks, where large sudden declines are unlikely. It also underperforms in strong bull markets: if Acme rises from \$100 to \$150, the trader would have been better off simply holding the stock, because the covered call capped the gain at the strike price.

The wheel is frequently discussed in the context of automated trading because it is entirely rules-based. The decisions (which strike to choose, which expiration, when to sell the next option) follow a mechanical process that can be codified and executed without human judgment on each trade. A system can monitor the stock price, select strikes and expirations, execute the trades, and manage the cycle continuously, including when the customer is not logged in, which is why the author mentions it. For [market makers](../../concepts/market-making/) and brokerages, automated wheel strategies are attractive because they generate consistent trading volume in options, whose wider spreads produce more [payment for order flow](../../concepts/payment-for-order-flow/) per trade than simple equity trades.

# Prediction markets and sportsbooks

See also: [Prediction markets and sportsbooks](../../concepts/prediction-markets/)

The column briefly refers to "agentic sports gambling" and to prediction markets. A prediction market trades contracts that pay out according to the outcome of future events, priced by the market's collective estimate of each outcome's probability, and a sportsbook sells bets on sporting events at odds that carry a built-in margin, the [vigorish](../../concepts/prediction-markets/#sportsbook-odds-and-the-vigorish). The author's point is straightforward: these businesses collect a wide margin on every trade, so an agent that places many bets continuously generates a great deal of revenue for them without meaningfully improving the customer's odds of winning. The customer's agent is, in effect, a tireless generator of revenue for the house.

# The broader context

The author draws on reporting to support the thesis, specifically two Wall Street Journal articles published on the same day. One covers how artificial intelligence threatens the "laziness tax" that banks earn from inattentive customers. The other covers Robinhood's annual event, where the company unveiled agent features including an in-app assistant that can research investments, build strategies, and eventually place trades autonomously through a feature called "Loops", which runs strategies repeatedly even when the user is not logged in.

The author also refers to a 2019 law review article by Rory Van Loo titled "Digital Market Perfection", which anticipated many of these dynamics before agentic artificial intelligence existed in its current form. That article explored how digital tools and algorithms could help consumers overcome information asymmetries and inertia in financial markets, essentially the same thesis applied to an earlier generation of technology.

# Summary

The column presents a two-sided thesis about the effect of agents on retail finance. Businesses that profit from customer inattention (banks, insurers, mortgage lenders) face disruption as agents eliminate the inertia these businesses depend on. Businesses that profit from customer activity (brokerages, market makers, sportsbooks, prediction markets) stand to benefit as agents generate continuous, high-volume, high-margin trading on behalf of retail customers. The author frames this as a dispersion trade, short the inattention businesses and long the attention businesses, and supports it with current reporting on both sides. The central insight is that the same technology has opposite effects on different parts of the financial system, according to whether those parts are built on customer inertia or on customer activity.
